{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["J7", "J8"],
      "pins": ["J7.1", "J8.1"],
      "nets": ["+12V", "GND"],
      "obs": "On the Power sheet, the +12V up-arrow feeds a vertical wire that joins BOTH pin 1 and pin 2 of each 4-pin fan header (J7 and J8), with junction dots at both rows; pin 3 carries FAN_0_TACH/FAN_1_TACH and pin 4 is a no-connect. No pin of either header is tied to GND.",
      "exp": "A 4-pin fan header's pin 1 is the fan ground; the specification lists only 'the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)' on the +12V net, so pin 1 must be GND (the return path for the fan motor and the reference for the open-collector tach signal).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet (power.kicad_sch): J7/J8 Conn_01x04 — +12V arrow wire with green junction dots at pin 1 and pin 2; pin 3 -> FAN_0_TACH / FAN_1_TACH; pin 4 -> NC; no GND symbol on the header."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Section 3 Inputs: '+12V net. Also on this net: ... the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2).'"}
      ],
      "impact": "The fan ground pin is driven at +12V instead of 0V, so a connected fan has no supply return (motor sees ~0V across it) and will not spin; the tach open-collector has no ground reference, so FAN_x_TACH reads are invalid and fan-stall detection fails. It also shorts the fan's GND wire to +12V, risking damage to the fan or the 12V rail.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic matches the specification on every value, rail, and interface (power mux, buck, heater high-side switch, thermocouple SPI/CS, USB CC, WS2812), and the documented quirks (D7 symbol type, unmonitored ~FAULT pin, placeholder LED colors, dead libraries) are not anomalies. The one deviation the drawing supports is on the Power sheet: the fan headers' pin 1 is tied to +12V (with no GND pin on the header) although the spec places only pin 2 on +12V and standard 4-pin fan pinout requires pin 1 = GND, which would stop the fans and break tach sensing."
}

